**Monitor Solution to the Dining-Philosophers Problem:**

**Problem Definition**:
Five philosophers sit around a round table with 5 chopsticks. Each philosopher alternates between thinking and eating. A philosopher needs both adjacent left and right chopsticks to eat.

**Monitor Solution Structure**:

```cpp
monitor DiningPhilosophers {
    enum { THINKING, HUNGRY, EATING } state[5];
    condition self[5];

    void pickup(int i) {
        state[i] = HUNGRY;
        test(i);
        if (state[i] != EATING)
            self[i].wait();
    }

    void putdown(int i) {
        state[i] = THINKING;
        test((i + 4) % 5); // Test left neighbor
        test((i + 1) % 5); // Test right neighbor
    }

    void test(int i) {
        if ((state[(i + 4) % 5] != EATING) &&
            (state[i] == HUNGRY) &&
            (state[(i + 1) % 5] != EATING)) {
            state[i] = EATING;
            self[i].signal();
        }
    }

    initialization_code() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
            state[i] = THINKING;
    }
}
```

**Working Explanation:**
1. **Mutual Exclusion**: Monitor guarantees that only one process executes monitor routines at any given time.
2. **Deadlock Avoidance**: A philosopher transitions to `EATING` state only if both neighbors are not currently eating (`state[(i+4)%5] != EATING` and `state[(i+1)%5] != EATING`).
3. **Synchronization**: If chopsticks are unavailable, the philosopher waits on `self[i]`. Upon calling `putdown()`, adjacent neighbors are tested and signaled if ready to eat.
